## {{senseid|en|race}} {{lb|en|transitive|or|intransitive}} To [[compete]] in a [[race]].
##: {{ux|en|The horse will '''run''' in the Preakness next year.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'm not ready to '''run''' a marathon.}}
##: {{ux|en|The car could not be repaired in time for the race and did not '''run'''. }}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[transport]] (someone or something), notionally at a brisk pace.
##: {{ux|en|Could you '''run''' me over to the store?}}
##: {{ux|en|Please '''run''' this report upstairs to director's office.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} Of a means of transportation: to [[travel]] (a [[route]]).
##: {{ux|en|The bus (train, plane, ferry boat, etc) '''runs''' between Newport and Riverside.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=Karl-Heinz Reger; Nelles Verlag Staff|title=Malaysia - Singapore - Brunei| publisher=Hunter Publishing, Inc| isbn=9783886189021| page=91| text=Small planes '''run''' between Alor and Langkawi. BUS: Express busses leave the bus terminal on the corner of Jl. Langgar and Jl. Stesyen for K. Kedah, {{nb...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|date=2013-04-15|author=Mary Ann Sternberg|title=Along the River Road: Past and Present on Louisiana's Historic Byway| publisher=LSU Press| isbn=9780807150634| page=62| text=The first steam ferry or tug, the Little Minnie, '''ran''' the river in the 1870s. When vehicles were to cross, a barge was affixed to the Minnie to carry them.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (a  vehicle) to  travel a route.
##: {{ux|en|Hundreds of cars '''run''' along this highway every day.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=2024|month=11|journal=Lackawanna Coalition Railgram|author=David Peter Alan|title=NJ Transit Runs Special Trains to “Railroad Museum [for a day]”|url=https://lackawannacoalition.org/2024/11/25/nj-transit-runs-special-trains-to-railroad-museum-for-a-day/|text=This year, NJ Transit allowed nonmotorists {{…}} to reach the event by '''running''' special trains every 2 hours 4 round trips). The location was the old Lackawanna Railroad freight house, about a 10-minute walk from the Boonton Station, which normally has service only during peak-commuting hours on weekdays.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260510175725/https://lackawannacoalition.org/2024/11/25/nj-transit-runs-special-trains-to-railroad-museum-for-a-day/|archivedate=10 May 2026}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[transit]] (a length of a river), as in [[whitewater rafting]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1979|author=United States. Forest Service. Rocky Mountain Region|title=Piedra River: Final Environmental Impact Statement & Wild & Scenic River Study| page=74| text=To put it frankly, if you people had to hire others to run the river and survey it for you, if, in short, you can't even '''run''' it yourself, why do think you can decide who is and who is not competent? River running, as has been{{nb...}}}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of fish, to [[migrate]] for [[spawning]].
## {{lb|en|American football|transitive|or|intransitive}} To carry (a [[football]]) down the field, as opposed to passing or kicking.
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 29, 2019|journal=Boston Globe|author=Chad Finn| title=24 thoughts on the Patriots’ loss to the Dolphins| url=https://www.boston.com/sports/new-england-patriots/2019/12/29/patriots-dolphins-recap-thoughts-analysis| passage=Then, on their second possession, Isaiah Ford '''ran''' for 11 yards after abandoning a flea flicker.  [...] The Patriots '''ran''' the ball just 27 times despite averaging 5 yards per carry.}}
## {{senseid|en|flee}} {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[flee]] from a [[danger]] or towards help.
##: {{ux|en|Whenever things get tough, she cuts and '''runs'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|When he's broke, he '''runs''' to me for money.}}
##: {{ux|en|When the alarm went off, the thief dropped the booty and '''ran'''.}}
##* {{quote-web|en|author=w:Howard Tayler|work=w:Schlock Mercenary|date=7 October 2018|url=https://www.schlockmercenary.com/2018-10-07|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260910030031/https://www.schlockmercenary.com/2018-10-07|archivedate=10 September 2026|accessdate=9 September 2026|text="Your wakeup sequence might have revealed your location.  '''RUN.'''"}}
## {{lb|en|figurative|transitive}} To [[pass]] (without [[stop]]ping), typically a stop [[signal]], [[stop sign]]{{,}} or [[duty]] to [[yield]] the [[right of way]].
##: {{ux|en|The car in front just '''ran''' a red light.}}
##: {{ux|en|If you have a collision with a vehicle oncoming from the right, after having '''run''' priority to the right, you are at fault.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|juggling|colloquial}} To juggle a pattern [[continuously]], as opposed to starting and stopping quickly.
# To flow.
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of a [[liquid]] or [[electric current]], to [[flow]].
##: {{ux|en|The river '''runs''' through the forest into the North Sea.}}
##: {{ux|en|There's blood '''running''' down your leg.}}
##: {{ux|en|The current '''runs''' to the toaster.}}
##: {{ux|en|My eyes were watering and now tears are '''running''' down my cheeks.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|figuratively}} To move or [[spread]] quickly.
##: {{ux|en|There's a strange story '''running''' around the neighborhood that you had a miscarriage last year.}}
##: {{ux|en|The flu is '''running''' through my daughter's kindergarten.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} Of an object, to have a liquid [[flow]]ing from it.
##: {{ux|en|Your nose is '''running'''.}} 
##: {{ux|en|Why is the hose still '''running'''?}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To make a liquid or electric current flow from or into an object.
##: {{ux|en|'''Run''' the water out into the pool.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''ran''' the electricity back behind the wall.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[liquid]]; to [[melt]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1717|author=Ovid|title=Ovid's Metamorphoses in fifteen books. Translated by the most eminent hands. Adorn'd with sculptures|section=Book the Third, The Story of ''Narcissus''|page=92|translator=w:Joseph Addison|origyear=a. 18 {{CE}}|passage=As Wax dissolves, as Ice begins to '''run''',|url=https://quod.lib.umich.edu/cgi/t/text/pageviewer-idx?cc=ecco;c=ecco;idno=004871123.0001.000;node=004871123.0001.000:6;seq=125;}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1729|author={{w|John Woodward (naturalist)|John Woodward}}|title=An Attempt Towards a Natural History of the Fossils of England|page=223|text=The ''Sussex'' ores '''run''' pretty freely in the Fire for Iron-Ores; otherwise they would hardly be worth working.|section=Tome I}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[leak]] or [[spread]] in an undesirable fashion; to [[bleed]] (especially used of [[dye]] or [[paint]]).
##: {{ux|en|During washing, the red from the rug '''ran''' onto the white sheet, staining it pink.}}
## To [[fuse]]; to [[shape]]; to [[mould]]; to [[cast]].
##: {{ux|en|to '''run''' bullets}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1718|author=w:Henry Felton|title=A Dissertation on Reading the Classics, and Forming a Just Style|page=6|text=But, my Lord, the fairest Diamonds are rough till they are polished, and the purest Gold must be '''run''' and washed, and sifted in the Oar.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Z5oDAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA6}}
# {{lb|en|nautical|of a vessel}} To sail before the wind, in distinction from [[reach]]ing or sailing [[close-hauled]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[control]] or [[manage]]; to be in [[charge]] of.
#: {{ux|en|My uncle '''ran''' a corner store for forty years.}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''runs''' the fundraising.}}
#: {{ux|en|My parents think they '''run''' my life.}}
#: {{ux|en|He is '''running''' the candidate's expensive campaign.}}
