# {{lb|en|of a [[number]]}} Convenient for [[#Verb|round]]ing other numbers to; for example, ending in a [[zero]].
#: {{uxa|en|One hundred is a nice '''round''' number.}}
# {{lb|en|phonetics}} [[pronounce|Pronounced]] with the lips drawn together; [[rounded#Adjective|rounded]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1959|year_published=1972|author=w:Anthony Burgess|title=Beds in the East|series=The Malayan Trilogy|page=421|passage="Supposing somebody sees you, with all those flowers too? Supposing somebody writes him a letter? Ooooh!" (a pure '''round''' open Tamil O.)}}
# Outspoken; plain and direct; unreserved; not [[mince words|mincing words]].
#: {{coa|en|a '''round''' answer}}
#: {{coa|en|a '''round''' oath}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1867|author=w:Matthew Arnold|title=On the Study of Celtic Literature
|passage=the '''round''' assertion}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Twelfth Night|act=II|scene=iii|page=261|column=2|passage=''Sir Toby'', I muſt be '''round''' with you.}}
