From {{inh|en|enm|rinsen}}, {{m|enm|rincen}}, {{m|enm|rencen|t=to rinse}}, from {{der|en|fro|rincier}}, {{m|fro|reinser}}, {{der|en|fro-nor|raïncer|t=to rinse, cleanse}}. Of contested origin. Possibly from {{der|en|non|hreinsa}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*hrainisōną|t=to clean, purify}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*krey-|t=to separate, divide}}. Alternatively from a dissimilation of {{m+|fro|recincier}}, from {{der|en|la|recento|recentare|t=to make fresh}}.

Cognate with {{cog|fr|rincer}}. From the Germanic verb are {{cog|da|rense}}, {{cog|no|rense}}, {{cog|sv|rensa}} (all “to clean”), {{cog|goh|reinisōn|t=to clean, purify, atone}}. It is related to {{cog|de|rein|t=pure}}, {{cog|got|𐌷𐍂𐌰𐌹𐌽𐍃|t=clean}}, and {{m+|en|riddle#Etymology 2|riddle}}. The Latin verb is related to {{m|en|recent}}.

